            "A patient is not only a list of risks. Most people facing an operation are <strong>afraid</strong> — of dying under the anaesthetic, of waking during it, of pain, of what will be found, of the scar, of losing a breast, a womb, a limb or the ability to work, and in Ghana very often of the <strong>cost</strong> and of who will care for the children and the farm. Anxiety raises the pulse and blood pressure, increases the anaesthetic needed, worsens pain and delays recovery, so <strong>psychological preparation</strong> is clinical care: find out what she already understands and what she fears, give information in small amounts and check it with teach-back, explain what she will see, hear and feel at each stage, teach the breathing and leg exercises she will be asked to do afterwards, be honest about pain and how it will be treated, involve the people she wants involved, respect prayer and the visit of a pastor or imam, and make space for the <strong>cultural</strong> questions — the family head who expects to be consulted, the fear that a removed organ will be used for ritual, the belief that an illness has a spiritual cause. Explain what the <strong>National Health Insurance Scheme</strong> covers for this operation and what it does not, in writing, before the day — a bill that arrives as a surprise is one of the commonest reasons a patient discharges herself against advice. Children are prepared through their parents and through play; the older person through the family that will nurse her at home.",
